                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "ec93bbcb-6a2f-3239-aa62-1e4c7721f752",
                        "entry_id": "6d4e588f-ce64-360b-9c2a-c8c9a19e5233",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Any marsh; marshy ground, swampland."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "75b6fdf5-3437-4c81-b450-13c091b99aac",
                                "sense_id": "ec93bbcb-6a2f-3239-aa62-1e4c7721f752",
                                "text": "The old tales told of these noble animals sighted padding across clodded fields or circling shrinking copses. Stalking the choking carrs."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d7f05a05-1a4d-45f1-9265-a5ed7a668c40",
                                "sense_id": "ec93bbcb-6a2f-3239-aa62-1e4c7721f752",
                                "text": "The marsh lands or ‘carrs’ that covered the low-lying floor of the vale could not be cultivated and the poorly drained flanks of the vale would be best used as pasture."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "874c04a6-7602-3fa7-8e15-bd5cf5d7e4e5",
                        "entry_id": "6d4e588f-ce64-360b-9c2a-c8c9a19e5233",
                        "glosses": [
                            "In particular, a marsh or fen formed when the litter of decaying reeds (e.g. in a lake) raises the ground level above the water, allowing more vegetation like sedges and then low bushes or trees to grow; a marshy woodland. (Compare marsh, swamp, bog, fen.)"
                        ],
